{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And of Gad he said: Blessed be He who enlarges Gad, etc.</b> Because in the preceding verse it says ״צִדְקַת ה׳ עָשָׂה״ and so forth, and to one who performs charity the term ״גָּד״ applies — גּוֹמֵל דַּלִּים (dealing bountifully with the needy) — and the Holy One, blessed be He, enlarges for him a portion of priesthood, and priesthood is a double portion, and by this merit ״כְּלָבִיא שָׁכֵן וְטָרַף זְרוֹעַ אַף קָדְקֹד״, as the Sages said (Bava Kamma 17a): whoever engages in Torah and in deeds of lovingkindness, enemies fall before him slain, etc. But he had not yet explained what the charity was that Gad performed; therefore he said: ״וַיַּרְא רֵאשִׁית לוֹ״ — for he himself saw fit to decree upon himself what he was not obligated to do by strict law. And what was it that he decreed upon himself? ״וַיַּרְא רֵאשִׁית לוֹ״ — for he decreed upon himself to go out at the head of the vanguard, for he relied on the merit that within his portion the lawgiver would be buried, so that his land would dwell in tranquility and in rest all the while that they were at war. ״וַיֶּתֶא רָאשֵׁי עָם״ — at the head of the vanguard, for by this he performed the charity of HaShem, just as the Holy One, blessed be He, performed this charity with Israel, as it says (Exodus 13:21): ״וַה׳ הוֹלֵךְ לִפְנֵיהֶם״ and so forth; so too they performed this charity. But ״וּמִשְׁפָּטָיו עִם יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — for according to the root of strict justice they were only required to march together with Israel equally, and by way of charity they accepted upon themselves to go out at the head of the vanguard." "And even according to the words of the commentators who explain ״וַיֶּתֶא רָאשֵׁי עָם״ as referring to Moses, the explanation is the same: for in every war Moses went before them, and so he commanded Joshua as well, as it says (Deuteronomy 3:28): ״כִּי הוּא יַעֲבֹר לִפְנֵי הָעָם״. Nevertheless, in all his judgments he was equal with Israel, for he did not seek precedence over them. And as for what is said, ״וְטָרַף זְרוֹעַ אַף קָדְקֹד״ — because skin for skin, etc.: when one strikes with a sword from above, the one struck raises his arm to save his head with it, and then he first cuts through the arm and afterward the skull, for the sword passes through both. Therefore he placed זְרוֹעַ before קָדְקֹד, and the word ״אַף״ is well settled in meaning."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:16:44.967555994Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}